Hi Kathy,

[b]Please stop work on this — the bug is ours, not yours.[/b] CSPP HEAP v3.0 is fine. We found the cause this morning, and we found it by doing what you asked: re-running with --noclean. That was the missing piece, and I'll explain why below because it may be worth knowing for other users.

[b]The cause.[/b] We run HEAP inside an Apptainer container, with the bundle installed [i]into[/i] the image. An Apptainer image is a read-only filesystem. airsb103.exe opens an unnamed Fortran unit inside the installation tree, and that write fails:

[code]At line 330 of file read_nl.F (unit = 40)
Fortran runtime error: Cannot open file 'fort.40': Read-only file system
FATAL: Error in HEAP_Retrieval.pl: Error running airsb103.exe[/code]

HEAP_Retrieval.pl aborts, and with it the NetCDF converters — hence no NUCAPS-EDR and no NUCAPS-CCR-AR. NUCAPS-OLR survives because main_cris_olr writes it on a different path. That is also why the run took 41 seconds instead of the documented 1–3 minutes.

[b]The proof, before the explanation.[/b] Same image, same test data, same command — the only change being the bundle bind-mounted from a writable disk instead of installed inside the image — produces all five families: 16 NUCAPS-EDR, 16 NUCAPS-CCR-AR, 16 NUCAPS-CCR, 16 NUCAPS-ALL-HR, 16 NUCAPS-OLR, in 1m 59s. We've since moved the bundle out of the image permanently.

[b]Two things in my original report were wrong, and I'd like to correct them on the record.[/b]

[list=1]
[*]I wrote that the word "retrieval" never appears in the logs, and concluded the retrieval step was never attempted. It [i]is[/i] attempted. That observation was about the wrapper log — the only one that survived, because without --noclean the working directory is cleaned up. HEAP_Retrieval.log and nucaps_retrieval.log are there in the failing runs too, and the first one has held the exact error message all along.
[*]I argued that since your own test case reproduced the failure, the problem could not be on our side. That reasoning had a hole in it: your test case rules out [i]our inputs[/i], not [i]our installation[/i]. Every single run — yours and ours — went through the same broken image.
[/list]

[b]It bit us three times, in three different ways, and that is the interesting part.[/b] Once we moved the bundle out of the image and onto disk, the same class of problem reappeared twice more as ordinary Unix permissions, because the deployed tree belonged to a different user than the one running the chain:

[code]sh: .../Common_Bin/main_nucaps_preprocessor: Permission denied        (no execute)
Fortran runtime error: Cannot open file 'fort.40': Permission denied  (no write)[/code]

Read-only filesystem, no execute, no write — three distinct failures, all in the same place, and [b]all three exited 0[/b]. The final one is now behind us: on our own NOAA-20 CrIS and ATMS data the package produces all five families, 14 granules each, in 1m 57s.

[b]One observation you may find worth acting on.[/b] The failure is detected internally: the log ends with FATAL: Error in HEAP_Retrieval.pl followed by

[code]ERROR MESSAGING SYSTEM IMPROPERLY SET UP. UNABLE TO E-MAIL ERROR MESSAGE.[/code]

but run_heap.bash still exits 0 and prints EXECUTION COMPLETED SUCCESSFULLY. In an unattended chain that is the worst possible outcome: our orchestrator recorded fourteen successful passes that had produced no soundings at all. If the wrapper propagated a non-zero exit status when a module reports FATAL, every one of the three failures above would have surfaced on the first run instead of the fifteenth — and we would never have opened this thread. A line in the installation guide noting that the installation tree must be readable, writable and executable by the account running the software — which rules out installing it inside a container image, and matters for any shared installation — would also have saved us two weeks.

[b]On running outside the container[/b], which you asked for: we could not. Our host is Rocky Linux 10.2 and the HEAP binaries are linked against the EL8 HDF5 (libhdf5.so.103, libhdf5_hl.so.100), which that release does not provide, so they don't load at all. That is precisely why we run HEAP in a Rocky 8 container — not as a layer between HEAP and a supported system, but as the way to give it the system your manual specifies. Worth knowing if other users start moving to EL10.

Thank you for the time you already put into this, and sorry for pointing it at the wrong place. The --noclean suggestion is what solved it.

Cheers,

Alejandro Sierra
LANOT — Laboratorio Nacional de Observación de la Tierra
Universidad Nacional Autónoma de México
